WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.440
The TV series based on the works of Stephen
King are a mixed bag.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.400
Some are incredible; others, not so much.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.080
So which ones are worth watching?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.080
We're leaving out some big ones here, counting
only the TV series or miniseries with three

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.320
or more installments.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.920
With that in mind, here's every Stephen King
TV series ranked from worst to best.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:27.360
Stephen King has long been outspoken of his
contempt for Stanley Kubrick's 1980 adaptation

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.360
of The Shining.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:33.640
In 2014, he told Rolling Stone that he considered
it not only a terrible adaptation, but a bad

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.500
movie in general.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.400
He explained:

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.100
"Obviously people absolutely love it, and
they don't understand why I don't.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.500
The book is hot, and the movie is cold; the
book ends in fire, and the movie in ice."

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.690
King took particular aim at how the movie
adapted the Wendy character, played with desperate

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.360
intensity by Shelley Duvall.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.680
The writer said of her portrayal,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.360
"It’s so misogynistic.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:59.400
I mean, Wendy Torrance is just presented as
this sort of screaming dishrag."

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.220
Kubrick's adaptation of The Shining wouldn't
be the last.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.220
17 years later, ABC released a three-episode
miniseries version of the story, starring

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.900
Steven Weber and Rebecca de Mornay.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.020
Over four and a half hours, King was able
to finally give audiences the story he always

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.360
wanted to see on-screen.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.700
While it was reviewed well at the time, the
years haven't been very kind to it.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:32.960
"There you are, Doc.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.300
Been looking for you.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.000
AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA.”

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.180
Turns out, four and a half hours is far too
much for this particular King story.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.510
The series drags, feeling bloated to fill
time, and Weber plays crazy in a way that's

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.180
more goofy than frightening.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.920
The whole thing plays out like an overcorrection,
and it winds up being far worse than anything

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.340
Kubrick could've ever produced.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:00.640
The 2002 series Rose Red revolves around a
dormant, haunted Seattle mansion.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.770
Psychology professor Joyce Reardon brings
in an unsuspecting group of would-be psychics

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.060
to serve as bait for the house so she can
somehow get back at her department chair for

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.040
taking her tenure.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.560
Aside from the house itself, which makes for
a decent horror backdrop, everything else

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.660
about Rose Red falls totally flat.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:23.040
Too much time is wasted on wandering about
aimlessly, calling out to each other, and…

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.500
well, wandering some more.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.520
The characters are awful stereotypes, and
Nancy Travis is horribly miscast as Joyce.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:35.080
All in all, this miniseries feels more like
a wilted rose than a red one.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.920
Stephen King's Golden Years was actually the
first original piece of work the author created

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.780
for TV.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:48.060
Airing on CBS in 1991, the series centered
around Harlan Williams, a janitor in his 70s

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.520
who is given strange powers after a lab experiment
goes awry.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.879
Harlan is doused in chemicals that not only
reverse his aging but, in later episodes,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.220
give him powers that affect the world around
him.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.460
In spite of its supernatural overtones, Golden
Years winds up playing out a lot like a typical

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.400
political thriller.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.050
The onset of Harlan's powers means that he
has to keep hidden from government agents

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:13.540
who worry their top secret experiments will
somehow be exposed.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.560
Harlan is a threat, so he has to figure out
not only how to keep the agency bad guys off

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.340
his tail, but also how to reverse the effects
of his chemical accident.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.620
While it may not be the best thing King has
ever made, it's at least entertaining for

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.260
the most part.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.740
Luckily, the show is also supported by one
of the better casts to have appeared in a

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:32.800
Stephen King series, too.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:39.340
Haven ran on Syfy for five seasons between
2010 and 2015, which actually makes it one

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.800
of Stephen King's most commercially successful
TV series.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:48.940
Based on the author's novel The Colorado Kid,
Haven follows FBI agent Audrey Parker as she

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.260
investigates supernatural occurrences called
the "Troubles" in Haven, Maine.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.360
As time goes on, the show delves deeper into
Audrey's mysterious backstory, as well as

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.660
the source of the Troubles.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.620
Haven took some time to find its footing,
and its first season was the lowest rated

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.440
of its five on Rotten Tomatoes, with the critics'
consensus being that the show was a "derivative

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.160
supernatural crime procedural."

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:16.699
As time went on, however, Haven improved,
shifting focus onto its supernatural elements

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.860
and character development rather than following
the standard police procedural formula.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:25.380
The show can go a little off the rails at
times, but as a whole, it works pretty well.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:31.360
Nightmares &amp; Dreamscapes: From the Stories
of Stephen King is an eight-part anthology

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.360
series that ran on TNT in 2006.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.180
It's based mostly off King's short story collection
of the same name, and each episode tells a

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.840
completely different tale, with a completely
different cast of actors.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.660
In true King fashion, Nightmares &amp; Dreamscapes
acts as a near perfect representation of the

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.900
author's track record with small screen adaptations.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.340
Some of the episodes are good.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:55.600
Others are not.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:01.580
The biggest problem is that Nightmares &amp; Dreamscapes
tries to do a little bit of everything, which

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:06.000
perhaps explains why it winds up finding success
only about half the time.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.080
What it does manage to do well, however, is
bring together a solid cast for each episode

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.680
— and the presence of those casts tends
to make up for some of the show's weaker storylines.

00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:20.420
Overall, however, Nightmares &amp; Dreamscapes
only serves as a sad reminder that you could

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.640
be spending your time watching a much better
anthology series.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.740
In a perfect world, Kingdom Hospital would
have been a miniseries.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:33.560
Instead, it was stretched into a regular TV
show that still wound up lasting only 15 episodes.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.339
It tells the story of the bizarre staff and
patients at Kingdom Hospital in Lewiston,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:42.920
Maine, a place that sits on the ruins of another,
recently burned-down hospital.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.220
The show gets pretty crazy at times, but it's
also genuinely creepy, too.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.840
By far the most interesting thing about Kingdom
Hospital, however, is the fact that it's the

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.160
first adaptation Stephen King took on that
wasn't drawn from his own material.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:01.660
In fact, the show was originally based on
a Danish TV movie by Lars von Trier.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.780
Still, King told The New York Times that the
show was

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:08.480
"...the thing I like best out of all the things
I've done."

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:14.140
The Mist could've been a great show about
human nature and the horror that comes with

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.420
facing your deepest secrets.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.179
Instead, it goes way too far with its visual
effects and totally forgets that in order

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.960
to make an audience care about a character,
that character has to be likable.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.120
"I think we should think less and pray more."

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:32.219
In the case of The Mist, which only made it
to ten episodes before being cancelled, the

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.420
characters are generally pretty terrible people,
and they really don't make it easy to root

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:36.920
for them.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.819
Add to that across-the-board mediocrity on
the part of the show's actors, and the result

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:46.480
is a series that fails to live up to the big-screen
version that came a few years before.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:52.420
On the plus side, The Mist does have one of
the creepiest settings of any Stephen King

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:52.920
adaptation.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.160
The fictional Bridgeville, Maine, is a beautiful
and picturesque location that morphs into

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.280
a living nightmare for its residents.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:01.720
Apart from that?

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.080
Not much to see, here.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:10.259
In 1999, ABC aired Storm of the Century, a
three-part miniseries about the residents

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.020
of Little Tall Island, set off the coast of
Maine.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:18.259
When a storm rolls in that brings the town
to a complete standstill, a mysterious and

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:23.520
deadly man named André Linoge approaches
the townspeople and promises not to harm them,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:24.640
for a price.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.120
In a sinister twist, that price turns out
to be one of the families' children.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.580
Storm of the Century is another of Stephen
King's original screenplays, as opposed to

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:39.500
an adapted work, and while it's a far more
successful attempt at the danger-in-the-weather

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:44.980
theme than that portrayed by The Mist, its
failure lies in King's inability to self-edit.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.820
There's really no reason this particular story
should've been a three-part series.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:51.560
If anything, it would've made a great feature
film.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.640
But there's only so far you can go with the
story of a demonic man in search of a protégé,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.840
especially when the bulk of people involved
in the story are pretty one-dimensional background

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:01.340
characters.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.800
Under the Dome has a fascinating premise.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:10.839
An entire town is suddenly blocked off from
the rest of the world by way of a huge, indestructible

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:11.340
dome.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.940
No one knows where it came from, the government
can't get through it, and resources quickly

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:16.840
start to dwindle.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:22.240
While there are sci-fi elements obviously
in place here, Under the Dome finds its most

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.740
success on account of Stephen King's ability
to find monsters in everyday people.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:31.860
Its first season was certified fresh on Rotten
Tomatoes, with the critics' consensus being

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:32.780
that the show was:

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:38.280
"...an effective and engrossing horror/mystery
with airtight plotting and great special effects."

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.820
But like other King series, Under the Dome
lost steam going into its second and third

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:43.320
seasons.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.300
Despite a number of twists and turns in the
story, there just wasn't enough story there

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.700
to warrant the number of years it stayed on
the air.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.760
Like many of Stephen King's stories, The Dead
Zone begins with a car crash.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.920
After waking up from a six-year coma, Johnny
Smith suddenly becomes able to see the past

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.440
and future of those he comes into contact
with.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:08.780
As The Dead Zone progresses, Johnny becomes
more involved with law enforcement, taking

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.600
on an advisory role of sorts to help solve
crimes.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:17.220
This was in 2002, prior to Haven and so many
other supernatural crime dramas we see on

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:21.480
TV now — so at the time, The Dead Zone was
a fairly original concept.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:26.500
Add to that a solid cast, and the show winds
up being a pretty well-rounded endeavor for

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.600
its six season run.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.760
If you were to flip on The Stand now, there's
a good chance you wouldn't be able to make

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.040
it through the first episode.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:39.920
The special effects in this six-hour miniseries
are very, very bad, firmly cementing it in

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.640
time as a product of early '90s television.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:48.560
If you can look past its dodgy effects, however,
The Stand is a series that won't disappoint.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:53.800
The Stand follows the journey of a group of
survivors in post-apocalyptic America.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.860
This series is a long haul, too — it's a
four-part miniseries that completely lays

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.660
out and then resolves the battle for humanity.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:07.220
But so is the Stephen King novel it's based
upon — at over 1100 pages, The Stand is

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.100
one of the author's longest books.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.160
Ultimately, the miniseries does the work justice,
but it'll take a little sacrifice on the viewer's

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.120
part to get there.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:22.180
In 2016, Stephen King teamed up with J.J.
Abrams to bring his novel 11/22/63 to life.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:27.100
The resulting series, which ran for eight
episodes on Hulu, tells the story of what

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:30.460
would happen if someone prevented JFK's assassination.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:36.120
James Franco plays Jake Epping, a teacher
who discovers a time portal in the back room

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:37.480
of his friend's cafe.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:38.760
"I’m telling ya.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.200
You shouldn't be here.”

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:43.500
“Well...

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:45.520
I'm here."

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:50.800
As Jake attempts to change the past, the past
pushes back against his efforts, and things

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.180
get complicated when he meets and falls for
a woman named Sadie.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:59.720
Not only is this a Stephen King series that
doesn't look like a cheap, made-for-TV movie,

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:05.540
but 11.22.63 is also well-acted and well-written,
and its eight episode length is exactly the

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.649
right amount of time to tell this story without
it feeling like it's dragging audiences into

00:11:09.649 --> 00:11:10.400
the abyss.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.680
Everything that Stephen King does best all
comes together beautifully in the little Maine

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.940
town of Castle Rock.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:22.360
In the creepy Hulu show of the same name,
André Holland plays Henry Matthew Deaver,

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:27.029
a man who returns to his hometown two decades
after leaving, having once been suspected

00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:28.680
of killing his adopted father.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:33.740
During his stay, he uncovers some disturbing
secrets buried by the town's officials, as

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.040
well as the supernatural forces behind them.

00:11:36.689 --> 00:11:41.319
Castle Rock has ties to the wider world of
King's written work, so fans will continually

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:44.280
come across hidden Easter eggs and in-universe
nods.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.520
But more importantly, the series is just really
well done.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:52.460
The cast is phenomenal, the production value
is high, and the result is a genuinely frightening

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:57.209
show that has introduced new generations to
the twisty, terrifying world of America's

00:11:57.209 --> 00:11:58.600
greatest horror author.

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:03.640
Mr. Mercedes premiered on the Audience network
in 2017.

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:07.760
The series tells the story of Bill Hodges,
a retired detective determined to solve the

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:13.720
case of "Mr. Mercedes," a killer who took
the lives of 16 people at a local job fair.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:19.860
When Mr. Mercedes sets his sights on Hodges,
a dark and twisted game of cat and mouse ensues.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:25.300
The show walks a fine line between police
drama, mystery, and horror, and the further

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:28.960
along it gets into its narrative, the more
sinister the plot becomes.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:33.379
Its premiere season is certified fresh on
Rotten Tomatoes, with the critics' consensus

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:34.220
being that it:

00:12:34.709 --> 00:12:39.540
"...propels its tense, creepy narrative with
quick-witted dialogue, strong characters,

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:41.120
and terrifying surprises."

